<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-normal-distributions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Normal Distributions</title>
  <p> 
    I defined a probability distribution in <xref
    ref="section-probability-theory"/>. There is one type of
    probability distribution that is, by far, the most prevalant and
    most commonly used: the normal distribution. In this section, I
    want to define the normal distribution, talk about its properties,
    and relate it to sampling distributions for hypothesis testing. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p> 
        The <term>normal distribution</term> is a bell-shaped
        probability distribution. It is also called the <term>bell
        curve</term> or <term>Gaussian distribution</term>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        A normal distribution is determined by two pieces of
        information: a mean and a standard deviation. There is exactly
        one normal distribution, one bell curve, for any fixed choice
        of a mean and standard deviation. 
      </p>
      <p>
        In formal mathematics, if <m>\mu</m> is the mean and
        <m>\sigma</m> is the standard deviation, the normal
        distribution is the following function.
        <md>
          p(x) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}
          e^{\frac{-(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <figure xml:id="figure-normal-distribution1">
    <caption>A normal distribution with mean <m>0</m> and standard
    deviation <m>2</m></caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        \begin{axis}[
            ytick=\empty,
            xlabel=$\frac{1}{\sqrt{8\pi}} e^{\frac{-x^2}{8}}$,
          ]
          \addplot [
            domain=-6:6,
            samples=201,
          ]
          {exp(-x^2 / (2*2^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*2^2))};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <xref ref="figure-normal-distribution1"/> shows a normal
    distribution: the familiar bell-shaped graph. The distribution in
    the figure has a mean of <m>\mu = 0</m> and a standard deviation
    of <m>\sigma = 2</m>. 
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-normal-distribution2">
    <caption>Three normal distributions</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        \begin{axis}[
            ytick=\empty,
            xlabel=$
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{\frac{-x^2}{2}} \hspace{1cm}
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi (3)^2}} e^{\frac{-(x-4)^2}{(2)(3)^2}}
            \hspace{1cm}
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi (5)^2}} e^{\frac{-(x+3)x^2}{(2)(5)^3}}
            $,
          ]
          \addplot [
            domain=-14:11,
            samples=201,
          ]
          {exp(-(x-4)^2 / (2*3^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*3^2))};
          \addplot [
            domain=-14:11,
            samples=201,
          ]
          {exp(-x^2 / 2) / (sqrt(2*pi))};
          \addplot [
            domain=-14:11,
            samples=201,
          ]
          {exp(-(x+3)^2 / (2*5^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*5^2))};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <xref ref="figure-normal-distribution2"/> shows three more normal
    distributions with different means and standard deviations. The
    mean is always the centre of the graph, and the standard deviation
    controls how wide or narrow the distribution is. Normal
    distributions can be either tall and narrow or short and wide. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me go through some of the most important properties of the
    normal distribution. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        The normal distribution is a unimodal distribution with
        equally sized left and right tails.
      </li>
      <li>
        The mean of the normal distribution is, indeed, the input
        <m>\mu</m>. 
      </li>
      <li>
        The standard deviation of the normal distribution is, indeed,
        the input <m>\sigma</m>. 
      </li>
      <li>
        The rules of thumb for standard deviation can be formallized:
        <m>68\%</m> of the distribution falls within one standard
        deviation of the mean and <m>95\%</m> of the distribution
        falls within two standard deviations of the mean. 
      </li>
      <li>
        The median of the distribution is also <m>\mu</m>. The normal
        distribution is a distribution where basically any central
        tendency you can define will be <m>\mu</m>, which is a very
        nice property to have. 
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    As I have discussed before, data that fits a normal distribution
    is called <term>normally distributed</term>. To repeat a bit of
    earlier discussion, it's valuable to be aware of both the ubiquity
    and the limitations of the normal distribution. Many kinds of
    data, over large sample sizes at least, term to be normally
    distributed as they are or normally distributed after a logarithm
    transformation. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        Physical features in biology like height or length tend to be
        normally distributed over a population (though this does often
        require a logarithm transformation). 
      </li>
      <li>
        Abilities and talents, at least as well as we can measure
        them, tend to be normally distributed over a population.
      </li>
      <li>
        Grade scores on exams tend to be normally distributed over a
        population. (This tends to be only visibule in very large
        sample sizes). 
      </li>
      <li>
        Rolling several dice and adding the sum produces something
        close to a normal distribution. This becomes closer and closer
        to normal the more dice that are rolled. (All the dice need to
        be dice with the same number of sides for this to work.) 
      </li>
    </ul>
    All of these normality assertions are only for usual data. For
    almost any category, you can find exceptions. Moreover, some
    measures are normal by definition, such as IQ. <sq>Normal by
    definition</sq> means that it is assumed, before any data, that IQ
    must be normal and scores are assigned to the population of
    test-takers to ensure that a normal distribution results. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Like the IQ situation, data can often be
    <term>normalized</term>: scaled and ajusted to make sure its fits
    a normal distribution. This is often done in testing, particularly
    for very large tests like national standardized tests and
    provincial graudation exams. Such grading of tests is called
    <term>grading on a curve</term>, the <sq>curve</sq> being the bell
    curve. Historically, this was quite common in many contexts,
    including university classes. Over recent decades, this has become
    a lot less common, since there are good reasons to expect classes
    not to be normally distributed. I'm sure you can still find
    university classes out there somewhere which are graded on a
    curve, but you might have to look a while. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In general, the assumption of normality is a dangerous one. The
    normal distribution is lovely to work with: it is the most well
    understood and commonly used probability distribution. It has very
    nice properties. It's really lovely if your data is normally
    distributed. But the assumption of normality is a major
    assumption, and making the assumption falsely will lead to poor
    data analysis and poor statistics. 
  </p>
  <p>
    All this discussion so far is about the use of the normal
    distribution as a model relates to matching a normal distribution
    to a set of real word data. This is certainly useful in general,
    but it's not actually the purpose of introducting the normal
    distribution at this point in the course.  For use, the normal
    distribution is useful because of how it relates to the sampling
    distribution defined in the previous section. There is a major
    result here, one of the most important pieces of mathematical
    theory that we need to do statistics.
  </p>
  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>Central Limit Theorem</term> says the following:
        under some technical continuous, the sampling distribution in
        hypothesis testing will become closer and closer to a normal
        distribution as the number of randomly generated samples gets
        larger. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <p>
    This is a lovely result for us. We need to understand the sampling
    distribution to do hypothesis testing. The central limit theorem
    says that we can always assume that the sampling distribution will
    be normal. This lets us use the well-developed theorey of the
    normal distribution to proceed with hypothesis testing. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I need to make a very important clarification here. The central
    limit doesn't say anything about the distribution of our original
    data or the population our sample is trying to understand. Our
    original data might even be about a categorical variable, which
    doesn't have a distribution at all. Even if it is numeric, our
    original sample data, or the numeric variable in the whole
    population, might have absolutely nothing to do with a normal
    distribution. The central limit theorem (as we are using it) is
    entirely about the <em>sampling distribution</em>. This is a very
    key distinction. The sample statistics we get from randomly
    generate samples, under the assumption of the null hypothesis, are
    a numeric data set with one numeric variable. The distribution of
    this data will look more and more like a normal distribution the
    more random samples we generate. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The statement mentions conditions. What are these? Mathematically,
    this can get quite technical, but being a little bit informal,
    these are the main idea of the condintuous.
    <ul>
      <li>
        The randomization process that produces the samples need to be
        actually random. 
      </li>
      <li>
        Each randomly generated sample needs to be independent of the
        others. That is, when I generate a random sample, I don't know
        anything about the other samples. 
      </li>
      <li>
        The sample size (of both the original sample and the randomly
        generated samples, since the randomly generated samples are
        all made the same size as the original sample) needs to be
        reasonably large. 
      </li>
    </ul>
    In the context of our hypothesis testing, we can basically always
    satisfy the first two conditions. If we are making random samples,
    we do want a real random process and we want each sample to be
    drawn independently. For the third condition is a bit trickier. In
    our randomization for the sampling distribution, the size the
    random samples is the same as the size of our original data set.
    If our original sample was small, then we already have challenges.
    The fact that the central limit theorem also might not work is
    just another of problem to add to the problems of small sample
    sizes. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now let's press on with actually using the central limit
    theorem.to add to the problems of small sample sizes. 
    In <xref ref="section-randomness"/>, we build a sampling
    distribution by doing a simulation and then comparing the
    simulation with our sample statistics. Now that we know the
    central limit theorem, we can assume that the sampling
    distribution is normal. But how do we calculate it?
  </p>
  <p>
    Well, a normal distribution is entirely determined by two pieces
    of information: the mean and the standard deviation. Therefore, to
    determine the sampling distribution, I need these two pieces of
    information. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        The mean will determined by the null hypothesis. 
      </li>
      <li>
        The standard deviation is tricker to determine. The
        calculation for standard deviation of the sampling
        distribution will be unique to each situation. If the sample
        statistics is a difference of means, there will be one
        calculation, but if the sample statistics is a single
        proportion, an entirely different calculation will be used.
        I'll talk about these as we go through the various kinds of
        hypothesis testing throughout the rest of the course. 
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    After this discussion of standard deviation, I need one more
    definition. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The standard deviation of the sampling distribution is often
        referred to as the <term>standard error</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    It's frustrating to have another term for something we already
    understand, but this is very common vocabulary for working with
    sampling distributions, so I will adopt it. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, once we have a normal distribution model of the sampling
    distribution, what do we do with it? Well, we use it to test the
    sample statistic. I'll leave the details of this to the next
    stretch outcome, but there are two steps.
    <ul>
      <li>
        From the sample statistics and the standard error, we can
        calculate something called a <m>z</m>-score. A <m>z</m>-score
        tells where the sample statistic falls in the sampling
        distribution. It does this using the standard error: the
        <m>z</m>-score a multiple of the standard error and identifies
        how far the sample statistic is away from the mean using the
        standard error as a unit. For example, a <m>z-</m>score of
        <m>1.7</m> means that the sample statistics is eactly
        <m>1.7</m> times the standard error away from the mean. A
        <m>z</m>-score can be positive or negative to indicate above
        or below the mean. 
      </li>
      <li>
        From the <m>z</m>-score, we calculate the <m>p</m>-value. This
        is, as before, a measure of the probability that our sample
        statistic was the result of random variance under the
        assumption of the null hypothesis. 
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-normal-distribution3">
    <caption>Sampling Distribution and Z-Scores</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        \begin{axis}[
            ytick=\empty,
          ]
          \addplot [
            domain=0:30,
            samples=201,
          ]
          {exp(-(x-15)^2 / (2*(6)^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*(6)^2))};
          \draw (18,0) -- (18,0.4);
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Let me visualize all of this. <xref
    ref="figure-normal-distribution3"/> shows the position of sample
    statistic in a sampling distribution. The mean of this sampling
    distribution is <m>15</m> and the sample statistic is <m>18</m>.
    It's <m>z</m>-score is <m>0.5</m>, meaning it is half of a
    standard error away from the mean. Its <m>p</m>-value is
    <m>0.309</m>, meaning that there is a <m>30.9\%</m> chance of
    randomly generating this sample or something larger. This is
    unlikely to be below any threshold of significance. 
  </p>
</section>
